(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-external-middle-ear-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"External and Middle Ear Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 55-year-old woman reports itching, fullness and reduced hearing in the right ear with no pain or fever. The canal is filled with a dark, sticky material and the drum cannot be seen. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cholesteatoma","Foreign body","Cerumen impaction","Otitis externa","Otomycosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has a live insect in the ear canal and is distressed by the movement. His drum is intact. What should be done first?","choices":["Irrigate with hot water","Wait for it to crawl out","Instil antibiotic drops","Fill the canal with lidocaine","Grasp it immediately with forceps"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — immobilise it before attempting removal. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has had a blocked left ear for five days following a head cold. She hears crackling when she swallows. The left tympanic membrane is drawn inward and moves poorly when air is puffed against it. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Otitis externa","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Acute otitis media","Cholesteatoma","Otosclerosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A retracted, poorly mobile drum after an upper respiratory illness is the classic picture. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man was struck on the side of the head. He had severe ear pain that stopped abruptly, and now has muffled hearing. Otoscopy shows a defect in the drum that does not reach its edge. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Otitis externa","Central tympanic membrane perforation","Marginal tympanic membrane perforation","Acute otitis media","Cholesteatoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pain stopping on rupture is characteristic. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 2-year-old girl has fever and ear pain with a red bulging drum. Her father asks what test will confirm it. What should he be told?","choices":["A swab of the ear canal","A blood culture","A computed tomography scan","An audiogram","No test is needed; the diagnosis is clinical"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 17-year-old swimmer has otitis externa with a canal so swollen that drops will not pass. What should be used?","choices":["Oral antifungals","A pressure dressing","A hearing aid","An ear wick","A tympanostomy tube"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has had five separate episodes of acute otitis media in the past nine months, each fully resolving in between. What is the appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue treating each episode as it arises","Start daily antihistamines","Arrange a temporal bone computed tomography scan","Fit a hearing aid","Refer for tympanostomy tubes"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. This meets the definition of recurrent disease. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has had bilateral effusions for six months with a documented hearing loss and delayed speech. Adenoid hypertrophy is obstructing both eustachian tubes. Which procedure addresses the obstruction?","choices":["Mastoidectomy","Stapedectomy","Septoplasty","Adenoidectomy","Tonsillectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 20-year-old rugby player took a blow to the ear this afternoon. The auricle looks bruised but not swollen and the landmarks are preserved. What should be arranged?","choices":["Discharge with no follow-up","An urgent computed tomography scan","Splinting of the ear now","Re-examination in twelve to twenty-four hours","Immediate drainage of the auricle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the collection can appear hours after the injury. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman has a perforation of the right drum with continuous clear serous drainage but no pus and no pain. Which subtype does this describe?","choices":["Chronic otitis media with effusion","Benign chronic otitis media","Chronic suppurative otitis media","Acute otitis media","Necrotizing external otitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — continuous serous drainage through a perforation. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy has a one-day history of fever and ear pain. The right drum is red and bulging. He is otherwise well and feeding normally. What should the parents be told about antibiotics?","choices":["Most episodes settle without them","They are always required at this age","They are needed only if the drum perforates","They should be given for six weeks","They should be given only after a culture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Most cases are viral, so a period of watchful waiting is reasonable. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has severe ear pain and conductive hearing loss after a flight. The drum is intact but immobile and she is in considerable distress. Which intervention gives immediate relief?","choices":["A hearing aid","Tympanoplasty","Myringotomy","Canal irrigation","Oral corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is reserved for exactly this severity. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy with acute otitis media develops swelling and tenderness behind the ear. Which structures has the infection reached?","choices":["The parotid gland","The nasal sinuses","The mastoid air cells","The cochlea","The external canal skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 16-year-old competitive swimmer has three days of right ear pain that is much worse when the ear is touched. The canal is red, swollen and full of debris, and there is a tender node in front of the ear. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cerumen impaction","Cholesteatoma","Barotrauma","Otitis externa","Acute otitis media"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pain on moving the tragus with a swollen canal. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has a confirmed cholesteatoma with erosion of the ossicles. What is the definitive management?","choices":["Oral corticosteroids","Surgical removal","Long-term topical antibiotics","Annual observation with imaging","Tympanostomy tube placement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, usually with tympanoplasty. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl has pushed a dried bean into her ear canal. The drum cannot be seen. Which action must be avoided?","choices":["Irrigating the canal","Using alligator forceps","Referring for removal under microscopy","Examining the other ear","Documenting the object"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — organic material swells when wet and lodges harder. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl has an earring back embedded in her lobule, which is red and tender with a little pus. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Oral antibiotics alone for two weeks","Observation for a month","Irrigation of the ear canal","Referral for a hearing test","Removal under local anaesthetic"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct; sedation is used if the child cannot cooperate. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with ear fullness is told to exhale forcibly against a pinched nose. She also has thick purulent nasal discharge. What is the concern?","choices":["It can trigger vertigo lasting weeks","It can drive wax against the drum","It can force infected material into the middle ear","It can rupture the tympanic membrane immediately","It can cause permanent sensorineural loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which risks acute otitis media. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man asks why his ears keep blocking despite daily cleaning. Which explanation is correct?","choices":["He produces an abnormal type of wax","His canals are unusually wide","Wax is a sign of infection","He needs to clean more thoroughly","The canal cleans itself, and cleaning pushes wax inward"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — impaction is most often self-induced. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with otitis externa is in significant pain. Which addition to his antibiotic drops addresses that directly?","choices":["An antifungal","A decongestant","A wax softener","An antihistamine","A corticosteroid"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — it reduces pain and inflammation. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 25-year-old scuba diver surfaces with ear pain that is now accompanied by spinning vertigo, ringing, nausea and reduced hearing. What does this combination indicate?","choices":["The facial nerve has been compressed","The condition is now purely psychological","The inner ear has been involved","The infection has spread to the mastoid","Wax has been driven against the drum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — rupture of the round or oval window adds these features. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman with bilateral otosclerosis finds her hearing loss disabling despite hearing aids. What surgical option is available?","choices":["Removing the mastoid air cells","Placing tympanostomy tubes","Repairing the tympanic membrane","Removing the adenoids","Replacing the stapes with a prosthesis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct; cochlear implantation is the other option. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy treated for acute otitis media five days ago now has worsening pain and is not improving. Which complication should be considered?","choices":["Exostosis","Mastoiditis","Otosclerosis","Presbycusis","Cerumen impaction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Failure to improve is precisely what prompts re-examination rather than a repeat prescription. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has been treated three times for external ear infection without improvement. The canal now bleeds when touched and contains an irregular growth. What is the appropriate next step?","choices":["Biopsy the lesion","A fourth course of antibiotic drops","Irrigate the canal thoroughly","Reassure and review in six months","Start oral antifungals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a canal carcinoma is often misdiagnosed as external otitis. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with ear fullness two days after a cold is due to fly in the morning. What should he be told?","choices":["Fly, and use antibiotic ear drops during the flight","Fly, and plug both ears with cotton","Fly, but sleep through the descent","Postpone the flight until symptoms resolve","Fly, but avoid swallowing on descent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pressure change across a blocked tube is what causes the injury. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has a canal completely filled with hard wax and reduced hearing. She has bilateral tympanostomy tubes in place. What is the appropriate step?","choices":["Reassure and review in a year","Refer to an ear, nose and throat specialist","Irrigate with body-temperature water","Irrigate with a bulb syringe at home","Prescribe antibiotic drops first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tubes move removal to a specialist. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has fungal external ear infection with visible debris throughout the canal. What does his treatment involve?","choices":["Observation alone","Clearing the debris and topical antifungals","Topical antifungals alone without cleaning","Oral antibiotics","Surgical canal reconstruction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cleaning matters as much as the drops. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has a suspected cholesteatoma but his drum is opaque and the extent cannot be judged by looking. Which study should be ordered?","choices":["An audiogram alone","Tympanometry alone","Computed tomography","Ultrasound of the ear","Plain radiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it defines extent, and is especially useful when the drum is opaque. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl has a firm nodule at the site of an ear piercing that has recurred once after excision. Which treatment must be avoided in her?","choices":["Radiation therapy","Intralesional steroid injection","Corticosteroid tape","Surgical excision","Avoiding further piercing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is used in adults but never in children. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has had a foul-smelling discharge from the left ear on and off for a year. The canal skin is normal and not tender. Behind a pocket in the upper drum there is white flaky debris. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cholesteatoma","Otitis externa","Chronic suppurative otitis media","Otomycosis","Cerumen impaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent discharge without canal infection, plus keratin debris in a retraction, is the giveaway. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
